      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantitatively, you can approach this by encoding neighborhood data into quantifiable metrics, such as average local unemployment rate or population density fluctuations over time. These features should then be incorporated into your model, perhaps using regularization techniques like Lasso to determine optimal weights. Alternatively, you could use hierarchical modeling to allow the community-level effect to influence individual borrower risk estimates. Finally, rigorously test the causal impact of these social features versus their correlation with existing predictors.",
